{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1993 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f59632691c8cee90049ebda33f0ccdd26f17776499dcccc1253e171bba4e091e" :edb/db-before-t 1992 :edb/db-after-t 1993 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535305 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:43:25.520Z" 13194139535305 true] [17592186124150 1100 "$$\n(x-2)(x+3)y'' + (x+1)y' + xy = 0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n\n- $x=0$\n\n- $x=2$" 13194139535305 true] [17592186124150 1100 "$(x-2)(x+3)y'' + (x+1)y' + xy = 0$\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n1. $x=-3$\n2. $x=0$\n3. $x=2$" 13194139535305 false] [17592186124150 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a$. Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2)(x + 3)y^{″} + (x + 1)y^{′} + xy &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + (x + 1)/((x - 2)(x + 3))y^{′} + (x)/((x - 2)(x + 3))y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{(x-2)(x+3)} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q(x) = \\dfrac{x}{(x-2)(x+3)}\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3$, since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0$. So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2$, since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535305 false] [17592186124150 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+xy & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x+1}{(x-2)(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{x}{(x-2)(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{(x-2)(x+3)}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{x}{(x-2)(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2,$ since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535305 true] [17592186124151 1100 "$$\n(x-5)y'' + \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}y' + \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^3}y = 0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2}$ and $Q(x)=\\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n\n- The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n\n- The point $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139535305 true] [17592186124151 1100 "$(x-5)y'' + \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}y' + \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^3}y = 0$\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The functions $P(x)=\\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2}$ and $Q(x)=\\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}$ are analytic about $x=5$.\n2. The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5$.\n3. The point $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139535305 false] [17592186124151 1145 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a$. Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-5){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 5)y^{″} + \\frac{x + 5}{x - 5}y^{′} + (3)/((x - 5)^{3})y &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + (x + 5)/((x - 5)^{2})y^{′} + (3)/((x - 5)^{4})y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5$, they are *not* analytic about $x=5$. So, statement I is false.\n- The function\n$(x-5)P(x) = (x-5) \\cdot \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2} = \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}$\nis *not* analytic about $x=5$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=5$. Therefore, statement II is false.\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535305 false] [17592186124151 1145 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-5){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5){y}^{″}+\\frac{x+5}{x-5}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{(x-5)^{3}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x+5}{(x-5)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{(x-5)^{4}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=5.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-5)P(x) = (x-5) \\cdot \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2} = \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=5$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=5.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535305 true] [17592186124152 1100 "$$\nx(x-2)(x+1)y'' + x(x-2)y' + \\dfrac{\\sin x}{(x-2)^2}\\, y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven the points $x = -1,$ $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2,$ indicate whether they are ordinary or singular points of the differential equation above. If they are singular points, indicate whether they are regular or irregular." 13194139535305 true] [17592186124152 1100 "$x(x-2)(x+1)y'' + x(x-2)y' + \\dfrac{\\sin x}{(x-2)^2}\\, y = 0$\n\nGiven the points $x = -1$, $x = 0$, $x = 1$, and $x = 2$, indicate whether they are ordinary or singular points of the differential equation above. If they are singular points, indicate whether they are regular or irregular." 13194139535305 false] [17592186124152 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a$. Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a$. Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-2)(x+1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 2)(x + 1)y^{″} + x(x - 2)y^{′} + (\\sin x)/((x - 2)^{2})y &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + \\frac{1}{x + 1}y^{′} + (\\sin x)/(x(x + 1)(x - 2)^{3})y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q(x)=\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3}\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where\n\n$$\nx+1 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich occurs at $x = -1$.\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where\n\n$$\nx(x+1)(x-2)^3 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich occurs at $x=-1$, $x=0$, and $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a *ordinary point* of the differential equation, and the *singular points* are\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\quad x=0, \\quad x=2\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1$, the function\n$(x+1)P(x) = (x+1) \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x+1} = 1$,\nis analytic about $x=-1$, and the function\n$(x+1)^2 Q(x) = (x+1)^2 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{(x+1)\\sin{x}}{x(x-2)^3}$,\nis also analytic about $x=-1$. Thus, $x=-1$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n- For $x=0$, the function\n$xP(x) = x\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x}{x+1}$,\nis analytic about $x=0$, and the function\n$x^2Q(x) = x^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{x\\sin x}{(x+1)(x-2)^3}$,\nis also analytic about $x=0$. Thus, $x=0$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n- For $x=2$, the function\n$(x-2)P(x) = (x-2)\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x-2}{x+1}$,\nis analytic about $x=2$, but the function\n$(x-2)^2Q(x) = (x-2)^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)}$,\nis *not* analytic about $x=2$. Therefore, $x=2$ is an *irregular singular point* of the differential equation." 13194139535305 false] [17592186124152 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-2)(x+1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x-2){y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(x-2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x+1}\n\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\nQ(x)=\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+1 = 0,$ which occurs at $x = -1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)(x-2)^3 = 0,$ which occurs at $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a *ordinary point* of the differential equation, and the *singular points* are\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\quad x=0, \\quad x=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x) = (x+1) \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x+1} = 1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^2 Q(x) = (x+1)^2 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{(x+1)\\sin{x}}{x(x-2)^3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x) = x\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\nx^2Q(x) = x^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{x\\sin x}{(x+1)(x-2)^3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x) = (x-2)\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x-2}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2Q(x) = (x-2)^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is an *irregular singular point* of the differential equation." 13194139535305 true] [17592186124597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342896 13194139535305 true] [17592186124597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342903 13194139535305 true] [17592186124597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342910 13194139535305 true] [17592186124597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342917 13194139535305 true] [17592186124597 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342924 13194139535305 true] [17592186124598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342931 13194139535305 true] [17592186124598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342938 13194139535305 true] [17592186124598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342945 13194139535305 true] [17592186124598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342952 13194139535305 true] [17592186124598 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343010 13194139535305 true] [17592186124599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342959 13194139535305 true] [17592186124599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342976 13194139535305 true] [17592186124599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342993 13194139535305 true] [17592186124599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343017 13194139535305 true] [17592186124599 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343034 13194139535305 true] [17592186342896 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342897 13194139535305 true] [17592186342896 1089 "q-342275" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342896 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535305 true] [17592186342896 1100 "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{(x+4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-4$\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=2$" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342896 1113 #uuid "7762c26d-e541-5c2b-81d8-f184a2bfa73b" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342896 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{x+4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-4,$ since both denominators at $x=-4$ equal zero. So, $x=-4$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342898 13194139535305 true] [17592186342897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342899 13194139535305 true] [17592186342897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342900 13194139535305 true] [17592186342897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342901 13194139535305 true] [17592186342897 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342902 13194139535305 true] [17592186342897 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535305 true] [17592186342897 1041 "selection" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342897 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342899 13194139535305 true] [17592186342897 1110 #uuid "72730227-41ac-5798-96b0-bc756c67f72b" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342898 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342898 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186342898 1111 #uuid "09073482-2005-5796-95f5-813b8c554e3e" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342899 1034 "I only" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342899 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186342899 1111 #uuid "fae7bd40-83c2-5785-921a-2dc6c471e78c" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342900 1034 "III only" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186342900 1111 #uuid "d9385934-3ecd-5d2c-8c47-55b0d029753d" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342901 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186342901 1111 #uuid "ecdb6aa8-2229-5c15-8a52-4f28fcaad68d" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342902 1034 "II only" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186342902 1111 #uuid "05eb6030-2e71-51ed-ae10-204baff663d4" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342903 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342904 13194139535305 true] [17592186342903 1089 "q-342279" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342903 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535305 true] [17592186342903 1100 "$$\n(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=3$" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342903 1113 #uuid "84f40794-4c8b-5b6f-9373-bde070195026" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342903 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-3}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{2x}{x+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-3}{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{2x}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-1,$ since both denominators at $x=-1$ equal zero. So, $x=-1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=3.$ So, $x=3$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342905 13194139535305 true] [17592186342904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342906 13194139535305 true] [17592186342904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342907 13194139535305 true] [17592186342904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342908 13194139535305 true] [17592186342904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342909 13194139535305 true] [17592186342904 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535305 true] [17592186342904 1041 "selection" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342904 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342906 13194139535305 true] [17592186342904 1110 #uuid "e7e7b23c-90f5-5f6a-943a-b792842b355a" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342905 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535305 true] [17592186342905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186342905 1111 #