 true] [17592186343005 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186343005 1111 #uuid "f6140130-9e3b-50b8-bac4-21bf92fd5a01" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343007 13194139535305 true] [17592186343006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343008 13194139535305 true] [17592186343006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343009 13194139535305 true] [17592186343006 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535305 true] [17592186343006 1041 "field-4" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343006 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343008 13194139535305 true] [17592186343006 1110 #uuid "de2f0cd5-6f0b-571f-9dab-e00ce6add106" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343007 1034 "an irregular singular" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186343007 1111 #uuid "38e26114-6a23-55b9-9925-1c8c1c1c47fe" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343008 1034 "an ordinary" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343008 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186343008 1111 #uuid "77c7b723-cc0d-554f-ad17-492adf4b3cb9" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343009 1034 "a regular singular" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535305 true] [17592186343009 1111 #uuid "59edb6b7-3331-51ff-9aeb-48604d2a76f6" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343010 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343011 13194139535305 true] [17592186343010 1089 "q-342479" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343010 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535305 true] [17592186343010 1100 "$$\n({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The point $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139535305 true] [17592186343010 1113 #uuid "38426d90-d9de-5708-ba65-07387f5cbef9" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343010 1145 "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=5.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-5)P(x)=(x-5)\\cdot \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{2x}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=5,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}Q(x)=(x-5)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{(x-5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=5.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343012 13194139535305 true] [17592186343011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343013 13194139535305 true] [17592186343011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343014 13194139535305 true] [17592186343011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343015 13194139535305 true] [17592186343011 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343016 13194139535305 true] [17592186343011 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535305 true] [17592186343011 1041 "selection" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343011 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343012 13194139535305 true] [17592186343011 1110 #uuid "06a