bf-a561-50d8-a014-5a2210f9d08f" 13194139535305 true] [17592186343017 1145 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-6)(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-6)(x+2){y}^{″}+x(x-6){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+2=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}=0,$ which occurs at $x=0,$ $x=6,$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,x=-2,\\,x=6.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{x}{(x-6)(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}=1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ Therefore, $x=-2$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=6,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-6)P(x)=(x-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=6,$ and the function\n\n$$